course: biology
lesson: 204
title: Goldfish and pond fish: the three-second memory myth
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: People joke that a goldfish forgets everything after three seconds, so swimming round a bowl is always a new adventure. It is a myth: goldfish can learn tricks and remember them for months.
explain: Goldfish are not tiny bowl animals either. They come from a dull greenish carp, can live for over twenty years and grow as long as a pencil or longer if they have the space.
explain: Watch the fish in a garden pond in winter: they sink to the bottom and barely move, because cold water slows their bodies down and the deep water stays a little warmer.
explain: Looking after goldfish well means a big tank or pond, clean water full of oxygen, and never letting them go into rivers or down the toilet.
fact: The three-second memory is a myth: goldfish have been trained to push levers, swim through small mazes and come to a sound at feeding time, and they remember for months.
fact: A small round bowl is too small for a goldfish: goldfish can live over twenty years and grow as long as a pencil or more, and they make lots of waste.
fact: Many places no longer give goldfish as fair prizes, because a fish in a small bag is stressed and often dies soon after.
fact: All goldfish, gold, white, black and spotted, were bred long ago in China from a dull greenish wild carp, and goldfish released into ponds and lakes can turn dull again and grow huge.
fact: Koi are colourful carp bred in Japan, and some koi have been reported to live for over a hundred years.
fact: In winter pond fish slow down and rest near the bottom, because the deepest water of a pond stays slightly warmer and does not freeze solid.
fact: Herons stand very still at the edge of garden ponds to catch fish, so pond owners use nets or give the fish places to hide.
fact: A goldfish gulping at the surface is usually short of oxygen; a partial water change and a filter help.
fact: Goldfish colour depends partly on light, so a goldfish kept in the dark can turn pale, and wild fish also change colour to match their surroundings.
fact: Flushing a goldfish down the toilet is not kind and does not set it free; released goldfish can harm wild rivers, and a fish swimming home to the sea through the pipes, like in Finding Nemo, is cartoon fantasy.
fact: Some chasing among goldfish is play or feeding, but too much is bullying; fish recognise each other and keep a pecking order, a ladder of who is boss over whom.
exercise: Someone says a goldfish has a three-second memory. What is true? | It is a myth; goldfish can learn and remember for months | It is true, they forget everything after three seconds, scientists say so | Goldfish have no memory at all | They remember only for one minute
exercise: What have goldfish been trained to do? | Push levers and swim through small mazes | Read numbers | Bark like dogs when they are hungry | Jump through fire hoops held over the water like circus dolphins
exercise: Why is a small round bowl a bad home for a goldfish? | Goldfish grow big, live long and make lots of waste | Goldfish hate round things | Goldfish need to see corners in their tank before they can fall asleep at night | Bowls are too cold for them
exercise: How long can goldfish live? | Over twenty years | Two weeks | One year at most | Three months
exercise: A goldfish is won at a fair in a bag. Why do many places no longer do this? | It is hard on the fish, which often dies soon after | Goldfish are too expensive to give away now | The bags are too heavy | Children prefer teddies, so the fish stay in the shop and never get sold
exercise: Goldfish come in gold, white, black and spotted. What did they all come from? | A dull greenish wild carp | A tiny golden shark | Clownfish | Wild goldfish of every colour
exercise: What can happen to goldfish released into a lake? | They can turn dull again and grow huge | They turn into koi carp | They die at once in all lakes | They turn into frogs
exercise: What are koi? | Colourful carp | Sea fish from Japan | Baby goldfish, before they grow | A kind of pond frog
exercise: Why do pond fish hide at the bottom in winter? | The deep water stays slightly warmer and they slow down | They are hunting worms that live deep in the mud at the bottom | The top of the pond is too bright | They are building nests for their eggs in spring
exercise: A heron stands by a garden pond. What is it doing? | Hunting the fish | Drinking the water | Guarding the pond | Waiting for the rain
exercise: How can pond owners protect their fish from herons? | Nets and places to hide | Feeding the heron bread instead | Painting the fish dull brown | Draining the pond every night
exercise: A goldfish swims to the top and gulps air. What is likely wrong? | The water is low in oxygen | The fish is hungry for bubbles | The fish is happy and playing | The water is much too clean
exercise: A goldfish turns pale after living in a dark place. Why? | Its colour depends partly on light | It is simply getting old, nothing else | It is so scared of the dark that it goes white | It ate too much white food from the shop
exercise: A goldfish is flushed down the toilet. What really happens? | It is not set free and released fish can harm wild rivers | It swims all the way home to the sea, just like Nemo in the film | It lives happily in the pipes | It turns into a sea fish
exercise: Goldfish chase each other around the tank. What could it be? | Play or feeding, but too much is bullying | Always a sign of love | Always a race | Always a sign they are ill
exercise: Do fish recognise each other? | Yes, they keep a pecking order | No, all fish look the same to fish | Only sharks can | Only koi can
test: «lol goldfish forget in 3 sec, so every lap is a new world» What is right? | It is a myth; goldfish remember for months | Yes, it is a proven fact that scientists checked | They forget in one second | They remember only their name and nothing else
test: «it's always waiting at the top when I walk in, coincidence?» What is likely? | It has learnt that you bring food | It is just a coincidence every single time | Goldfish cannot see people through the glass | It wants to jump out of the tank to meet you
test: «we won a goldfish at the fair, the little bowl's fine innit» What is right? | It needs a big tank with a filter, as it can grow big and live for years | A bowl is perfect for life | It only lives a week anyway | It needs no water changes, because goldfish clean the water by swallowing the dirt
test: «my nan's had a goldfish for 22 years, she's lying surely» What is right? | It is possible, goldfish can live over twenty years | Goldfish live only a year, so she must have had lots of them | Goldfish live only three months | Only koi live that long
test: In Finding Nemo a fish goes down a drain and reaches the sea. What happens to a real flushed goldfish? | It is not set free, and released fish can harm wild rivers | It swims all the way home to the ocean through the pipes, just like in the film | It lives in the pipes happily | It turns into a sea fish
test: What would happen if a pet goldfish was released into a big lake? | It could grow huge, turn dull and harm wild fish | It would always die at once, because lake water is far too cold | It would stay tiny and gold | It would swim back to the shop
test: «the pond's frozen on top, the fish are dead for sure» What is more likely? | They are resting slowly near the warmer bottom | They are frozen solid | They flew away | They are on top of the ice
test: «there's a big grey bird standing dead still by our pond, bless» What is it doing? | Hunting the fish, so they need hiding places or a net | Sunbathing | Guarding the pond for you against cats and foxes at night | Waiting for a bus
test: «why are they all gasping at the top, are they bored?» What is right? | The water is short of oxygen, so change some water and check the filter | They want to see you | They are practising jumping | It is a fish game in which the winner is whoever stays at the top the longest
test: Count them: 6 goldfish and 2 koi in the pond. How many eyes? | 16 | 8 | 12 | 32
test: Count them: a heron stands on one leg next to the pond with 3 frogs. How many legs in total, the lifted leg too? | 14 | 13 | 12 | 10
test: «the big one keeps chasing the little one non stop» What should you think? | Some chasing is normal, but non-stop chasing is bullying | It is always just a game, even if the little one never gets a rest | They are in love | The little one is the boss
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the memory of the goldfish? | Goldfish are capable of learning tasks and retaining them for months | Goldfish retain information for three seconds only | Goldfish possess no capacity for memory | Goldfish recollect solely their own name
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a small spherical bowl is unsuitable for the keeping of a goldfish. | The fish may attain considerable size and age and produces substantial waste | The fish is unable to perceive curved surfaces and therefore swims continuously into the glass | Spherical vessels are invariably too warm | The fish requires corners in which to sleep
exam: It is given that pond fish are observed near the bottom of a pond during winter. Ascertain the most accurate explanation. | The deepest water remains somewhat warmer, and the fish reduce their activity | The fish are engaged in the construction of nests in the mud for the eggs of the spring | The surface water is excessively bright | The fish have perished from the cold
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the disposal of a goldfish by flushing. | The fish is not thereby liberated, and released fish may harm wild waters | The fish is thereby returned safely to the sea | The fish thrives within the sewage system | The fish is transformed into a marine species
